    "narrative": "Our lineup for 2026-08-14 is built around high-upside leverage against mid-tier arms, headlined by **Tyler Soderstrom** (74.72) and **Mickey Moniak** (70.79). **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits from a massive 10% park factor boost and a favorable matchup against Rocker, while **Mickey Moniak** is positioned for a breakout game batting second against Roupp, bolstered by a significant 14% dynamic platoon advantage. **Seiya Suzuki** (66.61) remains a cornerstone, with his second-order spot against Liberatore providing high-scoring potential despite the L/L matchup. Meanwhile, **Josh Naylor** (63.65) and **Austin Riley** (62.71) round out the high-confidence tier; **Austin Riley** specifically overcomes his bottom-third order placement thanks to a 14% SP skill advantage and, crucially, a slow opposing delivery that keeps his stolen base probability in the green, providing that rare power-speed wrinkle we crave.\n\nThe closest call for today\u2019s roster was **Isaac Paredes** (59.26), who narrowly held his 1B spot despite the challenge of facing Kirby. While Kirby\u2019s 3.91 xERA is not insurmountable, it did suppress **Isaac Paredes**'s base projection, yet he retains his spot primarily due to the 9% platoon boost and his reliable middle-of-the-order role. Conversely, **Corbin Carroll** (52.80) illustrates our risk mitigation policy; despite his speed, the collision with an elite Sale (2.88 xERA) significantly hampered his outlook, dropping his efficiency score to a marginal range. We are holding tight on our IL-stashed assets like **James Wood**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**, while **Jose Caballero** remains sidelined, forcing us to lean on the current active depth. \n\nWe are monitoring the weather closely, as **Gabriel Moreno** (43.65) and **Corbin Carroll** are both facing the most difficult arm of the day in Sale. The 19% SP skill penalty applied to both players is the primary reason for their depressed scores. We have prioritized plate appearances for players like **Travis Bazzana** and **Cole Young**, who offer better efficiency against their respective opponents in Lambert and King. As we hunt for every stolen base to maintain our lead, we remain prepared to pivot if late-breaking lineup changes force a shakeup in the lower-order batting slots.",
